  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Lukose Shaji vsFoodInspector on 7 January, 2016.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Lukose Shaji vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 7Th Day Of January 2016/17Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1751 of 2006 ( ) --------------------------------- Cra 370/2004 of Additional District & Sesions Court (Adhoc-1), Thodupuzha Stc 1589/2004 of J.M.F.C.,Peerumedu Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellant/Accused:: ------------------------------------------------------------------- Lukose Shaji, Punnavila House, Elappara P.O. By Adv. Sri.N.A.Muraleedharan Respondent(S)/Complainant & State:: ---------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Elappara Panchayat Peermede Circle. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Eranakulam. By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 07-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.1751 of 2006 Lukose Shaji vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/113056045/ 1 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 7th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The accused in S.T. No.1589 of 2004 on the files of the Court of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class-1, Peermade has filed this Revision Petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Sections 2(ia), (a), (h), (j) and 7(i) read with Section 16(1)(a)(i) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 and Rules 28 and 29 of the PFA Rules. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 26.8.1998 at 1 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 600 gram of banana chips from the shop of the revision petitioner. After complying with the legal formalities, samples were taken. Thereafter, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P12 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for banana chips and was therefore, adulterated. It was further reported that the sample contained synthetic colour, tartarzine. On the request of the revision petitioner, another sample was sent for the examination to the Central Food Laboratory. Ext.P14 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample contained synthetic colour, tartarzine and hence, the food item did not conform to the standard prescribed for banana chips and therefore, it was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P19 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P14 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P14 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and Lukose Shaji vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/113056045/ 2 found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P14 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P14 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and Lukose Shaji vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/113056045/ 2 found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
